#type:     node
#context:  top
#internal: deadlinecloudscheduler

= Deadline Cloud Scheduler (Experimental) =

"""The PDG scheduler for AWS Deadline Cloud. Experimental."""

WARNING:
    This node is experimental. Its behavior, parameters, and the graphs it
    supports will change before release. Do not use it in production scenes.

== Overview ==

This scheduler cooks a TOP network as one AWS Deadline Cloud job. Each
schedulable TOP node becomes a step, and each work item becomes a task.

The job's settings, the farm and queue, and the queue's parameters work the
same way as on the Deadline Cloud ROP. The settings are stored on the node,
so they are saved with the scene.

When you create the node or load its scene, the node lays out its parameters
as this version of the node does, and keeps the queue's parameters, such as its
Conda packages, with their values. Any other spare parameter on the node is
removed too, because it can't be told apart from an older version's tabs.

== Cooking ==

You can start a cook in two ways. Both run the same checks first, and a
check that fails starts nothing and creates no job.

* Cook the TOP network as usual, for example with the Cook Output Node button.
  The scheduler refuses the cook if the scene has never been saved or has
  unsaved changes, or if a check finds a node it can't run, such as a node
  whose Cook Type is Service or In-Process. A refused cook stops before PDG
  starts anything, including a Service node's processes, and PDG then says
  "Failed to start scheduler", with the reason on this node's error tab. Any
  warning the checks find goes on this node's Warning tab, also when they
  refuse the cook. The cook uses
  the work items PDG already has, so after you change a parm on a SOP outside
  the TOP network, dirty the network or click __Generate__ before you cook, or
  use __Submit__, which does that itself.

* Click __Submit__ on this node's Shared Job Settings tab. If the scene has
  unsaved changes, it first saves them, or asks you to. Then it checks that
  the TOP network cooks on this scheduler, and runs the checks that need only
  the network's topology. If
  they pass, it generates the work items and runs every check on them. It
  stops generating as soon as a step or the job has more tasks than Deadline
  Cloud allows. Then it shows the job's farm, queue, name, priority, working
  directory, step and task counts, host requirements, and extra attachments,
  and any warnings. Click __Submit__ there, and the TOP network's display
  node cooks in the background, and the cook puts the warnings on this node's
  Warning tab. Click __Cancel__, and Submit puts them there itself. The job uses the settings on this
  node, and Submit doesn't change them, except that Always Save turns on
  automatic saving. When a check fails, Submit shows why in a popup. Once
  the TOP network has its PDG graph, Submit also puts the same text on this
  node's error tab, and any warnings on its Warning tab, so you can read
  them again while you fix the scene. The red error badge appears at your
  next click in the network editor. Submit does this for every check after
  it has the graph: a farm or queue that isn't set, a bad value on this
  node's tabs, a node whose Cook Type is Service, more steps or
  dependencies than Deadline Cloud allows, a generate that didn't finish,
  and every check on the generated work items. Only these show in the popup
  alone: the TOP network's Default TOP Scheduler isn't this node, the
  network has no display node, it has no PDG graph yet, or a step or the
  job passes a Deadline Cloud task quota while Submit generates. A Service
  node that the display node cooks is refused before the generate, because
  the generate would start its service processes. A Service node the display
  node doesn't cook is skipped, because nothing starts it. A generate that was canceled or that failed on a node
  leaves steps with no work items and errors on the nodes. Submit stops, and
  puts its text on the error tab too. The node errors can show a moment
  after the popup. Fix them, then submit again. When no node has an error
  yet, the text names the steps with no work items, because those steps may
  also make no items with their current settings.

Some checks need the generated work items, so the cook runs every check again
before it submits the job. Results stay on the farm: download them with
Deadline Cloud Monitor or `deadline job download-output`.

@parameters

== Shared Job Settings ==

Name:
    #id: name
    The job's name. Shown in the Deadline Monitor.

Description:
    #id: description
    The job's description. Included in the job template.

Priority:
    #id: priority
    The job's scheduling priority. The numerically largest priority is
    scheduled first.

Failed Tasks Limit:
    #id: failed_tasks_limit
    The number of task failures before the job stops running and is marked as
    FAILED.

Task Retry Limit:
    #id: task_retry_limit
    The maximum number of retries for each task.

Max Worker Count:
    #id: max_worker_count
    The maximum number of workers that run the job's tasks at the same time.
    0 sets no limit.

Farm:
    #id: farm
    The default farm of the AWS Deadline Cloud workstation configuration.

Queue:
    #id: queue
    The default queue of the AWS Deadline Cloud workstation configuration.

Login:
    #id: login
    Log in to AWS Deadline Cloud.

Logout:
    #id: logout
    Log out of AWS Deadline Cloud.

Settings:
    #id: settings
    Change the AWS Deadline Cloud workstation configuration shared by all
    tools, including the default farm and queue.

Update Queue Parameters:
    #id: update_queue_parameters
    Show the default farm and queue again, and refresh the queue's parameters,
    such as its Conda packages.

Submit:
    #id: submit
    Check the scene, confirm the job, and cook the TOP network's display node
    as one AWS Deadline Cloud job.

== PDG ==

Working Directory:
    #id: working_dir
    The cook's working directory on this workstation. PDG writes its job
    scripts and outputs below it, and each step receives it through job
    attachments.

Debug Level:
    #id: debug_level
    How much the cook writes to its log directory. __Standard__ writes the
    files every cook writes. __Graph dump and item details__ adds a dump of the
    graph and the details of each work item.

Validate Graph:
    #id: validate_graph
    Refuse a graph this scheduler cannot cook correctly, before anything is
    submitted, and name what to change. When off, each refusal is shown as a
    warning and the cook goes ahead. Turn it off only when you know the check
    does not apply, for example when every machine shares one file system. The
    check for an unsaved scene stays on.

Automatically save scene (.hip) file:
    #id: auto_save_hip
    Save the scene without asking when you click __Submit__ with unsaved
    changes. When it's off, Submit asks, and __Always Save__ turns it on.

Include Adaptor Wheels:
    #id: include_adaptor_wheels
    Install custom builds of the adaptor on the workers.

Adaptor Wheels:
    #id: adaptor_wheels
    The directory that holds the adaptor wheels.

== Job Attachments ==

The cook attaches the saved scene and the files its parms name on every cook.
Use these lists only for what that scan can't find.

Input Filenames:
    #id: input_filenames
    Files the job needs that the scene doesn't name in a parm, such as a file a
    Python node opens. They are attached next to the scene's own files. A
    listed file that doesn't exist stops the cook.

Input Directories:
    #id: input_directories
    Folders whose files are all attached as inputs. A folder that is a drive or
    a home directory, or holds one, stops the cook.

Output Directories:
    #id: output_directories
    Folders outside the working directory that tasks write to. Each task
    uploads what it writes there, so the steps below and Deadline Cloud
    Monitor can fetch it. A folder that holds the working directory, or is
    inside it, stops the cook. On Linux and macOS, so does a folder that
    shares only / with the working directory or another output folder, such
    as /tmp/out with a working directory under /opt, when the queue's storage
    profile gives the two the same LOCAL location or none. Put it under the
    working directory's top-level folder, or ask your farm admin to give the
    two different LOCAL locations in the storage profile. On Windows, a
    folder on another drive, or one that shares only the drive with the
    working directory, isn't refused.

== Host Requirements ==

The requirements apply to every step of the job, as on the Deadline Cloud ROP.
Each number is -1 when it's not set.

Run On:
    #id: hr_mode
    __All available worker hosts__, or only __Worker hosts that meet the
    following requirements__.

Operating System:
    #id: hr_os
    The operating systems a worker may run. Leave empty for any.

CPU Architecture:
    #id: hr_cpu_arch
    The CPU architectures a worker may have. Leave empty for any.

vCPUs Min:
    #id: hr_vcpu_min
    The fewest vCPUs a worker must have. The other minimum and maximum fields
    work the same way. Memory and GPU memory are in GiB.

Custom Amounts:
    #id: hr_amounts
    Amounts your fleet defines, each with a name and a minimum or maximum.

Custom Attributes:
    #id: hr_attributes
    Attributes your fleet defines, each with a name, any of or all of, and
    comma-separated values.
